  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Monday..\n\nWinds have begun increasing along the western foothills as a\nshortwave approaches, causing surface pressure gradients to \ntighten. Southwest winds gusting into the 20s to 30s mph have been\nobserved around Livingston and Nye. The aforementioned shortwave \nwill push through the area during the day today, bringing breezy \nwesterly winds and showers (10-30% chance, greatest over the \nmountains). Widespread wind gusts of 20 to 40 mph can be expected,\nwith areas along the western foothills seeing wind gusts of 40 to\n55 mph, strongest near Harlowton. Winds look to peak during the \nlate morning/early afternoon timeframe. Current probabilities of \nwind gusts exceeding 60 mph are around 40% near Harlowton, to 25% \nat Livingston. Winds will decrease late this afternoon into the \nevening hours, remaining somewhat breezy overnight near Livingston\nand Nye.\n\nWednesday through Saturday, upper ridging builds over the region\nbringing warm and dry conditions. Highs will range from the upper\n50s to 60s most days, well above normal for this time of year. \nHeading into Sunday and Monday, ensembles and cluster analysis \nshow lower heights building into the region as upper troughing \ngradually pushes inland over the western US, bringing a breakdown \nof the upper ridge. This brings the potential for cooler and more\nunsettled conditions for the week ahead. STP",
